From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: athexweb3 <athexweb3@nirapod.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:22:54 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] armv8a: add Advanced SIMD permutations and element moves

EXT, REV32, REV64, TRN1, TRN2, ZIP1, ZIP2, UZP1 and UZP2 rearrange the
elements of 128-bit vectors. DUP fills a vector and INS sets one
element, from a general-purpose register (#VDUP, #VINS) or from a
vector element (#VDUPE, #VINSE), and UMOV moves an element to a
general-purpose register. SHA-2 code uses REV32 and REV64 for the
message byte order and EXT to rotate elements.

Element indices, and the byte position of EXT, are immediates, checked
at assembly generation against the number of elements of the
arrangement. That check is the only guard for EXT: llvm-mc accepts
positions above 15 and encodes them modulo 16. The semantics follow
DDI 0487 M.d, C7.2, and all these instructions are
data-independent-time (C5.2.4).

An operand description pairs a register with its arrangement and can't
name a single element. So a table next to the instruction descriptions
gives, for DUP (element), INS and UMOV, the positions of a register and
its index, and the printer prints that pair as Vn.Ts[i]. Only the
128-bit forms are provided, with no scalar D, S, H or B views, and the
instructions are intrinsics only.

Co-authored-by: orvenweb3 <orvenweb3@gmail.com>
